# Phase advancer

`ticket-starter` covers the beginning of a ticket's life — routing,
worktree, initial phase state. Nothing else was covering what happens
*after*: an artifact gets approved, and someone has to notice the gate
cleared and move the state machine forward. This is that someone.

## When to use this

- Immediately after you (the agent) set `status: approved` on any
  gating artifact — because the user said "looks good, approved" or
  equivalent, not because you decided it yourself. Approval is always
  a human call; this skill only reacts to one that already happened.
  See "Recording an approval" below for the three fields that have to
  be set, every time, without exception.
- Whenever the user asks what phase they're in, or whether they can
  move on to the next thing.
- Proactively, before ending a working session on a ticket, so state
  doesn't go stale between sessions.

## Recording an approval — always three fields

An approval this client's gate engine accepts is:

- `status: approved`
- `approved_by: <name>`
- `approval_evidence: <link>` — where the human actually approved

`require_approval_evidence` is on for this client
(`.pdlc/phase-graph.yaml`). Without a non-empty `approval_evidence` the
gate stays blocked no matter what `status` says, so setting only the
first two doesn't move anything — it just produces a file that looks
approved and a gate that isn't.

**This includes in-session approvals.** "Looks good, approved" typed to
you in this session is not itself the evidence: a name typed into a file
is not proof that the named person approved, which is the entire point
of the field. So when the approval is relayed ("the PO approved this in
the Jira comment", "release manager signed off over Slack"), get the
link to that comment/message/email — and when the approval is said
directly to you, get a link too. Either:

- If this session has an MCP connection to Jira, post the approval as a
  comment on the ticket (or fetch the existing one) and use its
  permalink. Prefer this over asking — same fetch-over-ask preference as
  `jira-intake`/`ticket-starter`.
- Otherwise, ask the user for the link, and say why: real approvers
  (product owner, compliance officer, release manager) routinely have no
  repository, GitHub, Cursor or Claude access at all — they approve
  where they already work, and this field is what makes that checkable
  rather than transcribed.

Do not set `status: approved` with `approval_evidence` left blank or
filled with a placeholder, and do not advance and hope. If the user
can't produce a link right now, leave the artifact as a draft and say
what's missing.

## A gate that needs two signatures

Most gates need one. A gate whose `approval:` in
`.pdlc/phase-graph.yaml` names a `quorum` or `tier` strategy needs more,
and its artifact records them as a list instead of the three scalars:

```yaml
status: approved
approvals:
  - role: product_owner
    by: maria.lopez
    status: approved
    evidence: "https://client.atlassian.net/browse/BANK-1234?focusedCommentId=104822"
  - role: engineering_lead
    by: juan.perez
    status: pending
```

Three things to get right:

- **Every entry needs its own `evidence` link.** One link does not cover
  two people. A second signature without one leaves the gate shut, the
  same as a first one without one.
- **When the check reports `partial`, say who it is waiting on, in those
  words, and name the person if you know them.** The second approver
  usually has no idea it is their turn, and "not approved yet" reads
  like nobody has looked at it. That is the whole reason `partial` is a
  distinct state.
- **`DECLINED` is not blocked.** Somebody with the authority to approve
  said no. Report it as a decision that needs a conversation, never as a
  missing signature, and do not go looking for a different approver.

Never add an entry for a decision you did not witness, and never edit
someone else's entry. An entry for a role the gate did not ask for does
not count toward it — the check says so rather than quietly ignoring it.

## What counts as an approval, and what doesn't

The three fields above are the *form* of an approval. This is the
*substance*: whether the thing a human said was an approval at all.
Evidence proves **where** an approval happened; this rule governs
**whether** one happened. Both have to hold.

**Counts as an approval** — an explicit, unambiguous yes about *this*
artifact and *this* gate: "yes, approved", "I approve this", "go ahead,
this looks good to ship", a literal "yes" to the direct question "can I
record this as your approval of `spec.md`?".

**Does not count**, ever:

| What was said | Why it isn't an approval |
|---|---|
| "looks good" about one artifact while others are still pending review | It approves that one thing at most, not the gate |
| "ok" in reply to a different question | The answer wasn't about this gate |
| silence, or no reply | Nothing was said |
| an emoji reaction | Not attributable, not specific |
| "sure" / "go ahead" with no clear referent | You'd be choosing what it referred to |
| any answer to a question that wasn't specifically about this gate | Same |

**Read the exact words back before recording.** Tell the human the
literal words you are about to record — "I'm going to record your
approval as: 'yes, approved — ship it'" — and record only once they
confirm that is accurate. This is what catches a paraphrase they never
actually said.

Record their words verbatim; never paraphrase them into a verdict they
did not give, and never draft the verdict for them. When you are
genuinely unsure whether something counted, **ask directly**. Never
infer an approval from context, from momentum, or from the fact that
everything else is ready.

## Where the state and artifacts live

Everything for a ticket lives in that ticket's own directory inside its
worktree: `pdlc/<TICKET-ID>/` — `intent.md`, `scope.md`, `spec.md`,
`plan.md`, `release.md`, `phase-state.json`, `memory/runs/`. Not the repo
root: root-level artifacts made every later ticket inherit the previous
ticket's approvals, and made concurrent tickets conflict.

`status`, `advance` and `gate-check` need no `--project` when you run
them anywhere inside a checkout that has exactly one in-flight ticket
directory — they find it. With more than one they list the candidates
and ask for `--project pdlc/<ID>`; pass the one the user is actually
working on rather than picking for them. An explicit `--project`
pointing at a directory with no `phase-state.json` is an error, not a
silent fallback, so if you see that, you pointed at the wrong directory.

The gate engine also checks that each artifact's frontmatter `id` equals
the ticket id in `phase-state.json`. If a gate reports an id mismatch,
an artifact was copied in from another ticket — fix the artifact, don't
edit `phase-state.json` to match it.

## What to do

1. Run `node .pdlc/bin/spine.js status` from anywhere in the ticket's
   worktree (add `--project pdlc/<ID>` if it asks, because more than one
   ticket is in flight there). Read the result, don't guess at it.
2. If it says `gate: NOT CLEAR`, tell the user what's blocking it in
   plain language, **including who has to approve it** — `status` names
   the required role for each blocked artifact, so pass that through:
   "spec.md is drafted but not approved — Design needs the engineering
   lead's sign-off before it can close." Don't make the user go and
   look up which role owns which artifact.

   If the blocker is an **escalation** warning (a fast-tracked change
   that's grown past its threshold — see `.pdlc/phase-graph.yaml`), say
   so specifically: "this fast-track fix has grown to over 400 changed
   lines — consider re-routing to standard for a real Design pass" —
   that's a scope signal for the human to act on, not a
   missing-approval nag.

   If the blocker is a missing `approval_evidence` on an artifact that
   already says `status: approved`, say that plainly too — it is the
   most common reason a gate stays shut when everyone thinks it's open.
   See "Recording an approval" above.
3. If it says `gate: CLEAR`, run `node .pdlc/bin/spine.js advance` (same
   `--project` rule) and report the new phase: "Design is done — we're
   in Develop now, next up is `plan.md`."
4. If advancing lands on a **post-hoc** phase (visible in `status`'s
   output), say so explicitly — "we're now in the post-hoc plan
   phase for this emergency fix, since it skipped ahead to Develop
   originally" — the paperwork is still owed, just after the fact.
5. After a successful `advance`, and after recording an approval that
   did **not** clear the gate, run the `jira-sync` discipline so the
   ticket's Jira issue mirrors the new phase or the still-open gate. It
   is a no-op unless this repo opted in (`pdlc/jira-status-map.yaml`),
   and it is fail-open: if Jira is unreachable, say so and carry on —
   it never blocks the local flow.

## Who approves what

`status` tells you the role; this is the mapping it's reporting from
(`.pdlc/phase-graph.yaml`), and the client-side name for each role:

| Phase | Artifact | Approval role | Who that is here |
|---|---|---|---|
| plan | `intent.md` | `product_owner` | a product definer |
| define | `scope.md` | `product_owner` | a product definer |
| design | `spec.md` | `engineering_lead` | the builder leader |
| design | `compliance-signoff.md` (optional) | `compliance_officer` | compliance |
| develop | `plan.md` | `engineering_lead` | the builder leader |
| launch | `release.md` | `release_manager` | release management |

On the **emergency** track the order is engineering lead (`plan.md`),
then release manager (`release.md`), then — post hoc — the product owner
on `intent.md`. Don't treat the post-hoc product-owner approval as
optional just because the fix already shipped.

## Failure modes

| Situation | Correct behaviour |
|---|---|
| The user says "looks good" while another required artifact is still open | Not an approval of the gate. Ask about each open artifact by name. |
| You are unsure whether what they said counted as an approval | Ask directly, and read the words back before recording. Never infer. |
| An artifact says `status: approved` but `approval_evidence` is empty | Say plainly that the gate is still shut and why. Don't advance and hope. |
| The gate reports CLEAR | Advance, then say what phase we're in now. Leaving a cleared gate un-advanced silently is the failure this skill exists to prevent. |
| `status` output is long or full of internal wording | Report it in plain language in the user's own language — never paste raw command output at them. |
| `jira-sync` fails or Jira is unreachable | Say so in one line and carry on. It never blocks the local flow. |

## What this explicitly does not do

- Doesn't decide whether something is approved — it only acts after a
  human already said so, recorded as `status: approved` +
  `approved_by` + `approval_evidence` in the artifact itself.
- Doesn't draft the next phase's artifact — that's the phase module
  that owns the slot (`jira-intake` for `intent.md`, `scope-writer` for
  `scope.md`, `spec-writer` for `spec.md`, `plan-writer` for `plan.md`,
  `pr-review` for `release.md`), or a human doing it directly. This
  only moves the state machine once the current phase's gate is
  provably clear.
